  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA closed upper low over Quebec will continue to drift northeast\nand open up through the day. Despite the weakening system, \ncooler temps aloft will lead to unstable conditions by peak \nheating. Convection will be scattered, similar to yesterday. \nForecast soundings do show quite a bit of dry air, so any shower\nor storm may have some gusty winds. However they do show some \nwarmer mid to upper level air than yesterday, so the small hail \npotential is likely a little bit lower.\n\nThen surface ridging tries to poke into the region for Tue\nnight. This should allow for cooler temps in radiational\ncooling, especially in the northern valleys. Currently NBM\nguidance has some upper 40s, which closely matches MOS guidance.\nSo at this time I see no reason for changes. There will also\nlikely be more valley fog, which I have added. Fair weather is\nexpected Wed as return flow begins to set up ahead of the next\ntrof.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA weakening cold front approaches New England during the day on\nThursday as low pressure tracks through Canada. A weak warm \nfront approaches on Thursday as well, bringing a slight increase\nin moisture ahead of the cold front.\n\nThe front looks to be decaying as it approaches, so shower and \nstorm activity associated with the front will likely be \nscattered and more focused across western areas Thursday \nafternoon. Shower chances continue through the overnight, and \nthen a stronger front likely pushes through on Friday and ushers\nin a drier airmass. With the front on Friday, scattered and \nshowers and storms will be possible again during the daytime, \nwith the best chance across the higher terrain.\n\nTemperatures look to be pretty similar behind the front as \nahead of it, with widespread 70s to near 80s degrees from \nThursday through the weekend. There will be a noticble \ndifference in the humidity over the weekend however, with dew \npoints dropping back into the 50s to upper 40s behind the front.\nHigh pressure for the weekend brings seasonably cool nights, \nand abundant sunshine for the daytime.\n\nBy early next week the high moves off the East Coast, bringing \na warmer and more moist return flow. Chances for showers and \nstorms increase again early next week with the next cold front \npossibly approaching New England by Tuesday.",
